\section{Inner and outer folds}{

The inner folds should hold points out as the outer ones do.  A
cross-validated score is the error at the distance the folds put between
the held-out points and their training data, so nested selection chooses
the variables that predict best at the inner folds' distance.  Random
inner folds inside blocked outer folds choose for predicting among the
samples, where a variable that only tracks nearby values of the response
can win, and the outer loop then scores the chosen set at the blocks'
distance, for which it was not chosen.  \code{method} therefore defaults
to \code{"block_kfold"}, as the folds of the \code{cv_*()} functions do,
and logs a caution (not an R warning) when set to \code{"random_kfold"}.
}

\section{When a variable is accepted}{

Each candidate set is scored by cross-validation on the same inner folds,
and the candidate with the best pooled score is the one considered.  Under
\code{rule = "se"} it is accepted when two things hold: the pooled score
improves by more than \code{tol}, and the mean of the per-fold
improvements (the metric computed in each fold for the set with and
without the candidate, and differenced) exceeds \code{se_mult} times the
standard error of that mean across the folds.  This is the
one-standard-error idea of Breiman et al. (1984) applied to a step: a gain
the folds do not agree on is not evidence that the variable predicts.  The
fold is the unit because under spatially blocked folds the errors within
a block are correlated, so a standard error over rows would understate
the noise.  Against \code{rule = "tol"}, which accepts any pooled gain
above \code{tol}, the rule adds fewer noise variables and costs power on
weak predictors.  With few folds the standard error is itself noisy, so
raise \code{k} before lowering \code{se_mult}.  \code{history} records each
candidate's pooled gain, mean per-fold gain and its standard error, and
\code{fold_scores} the per-fold scores themselves, so every decision can be
checked.
}

\section{The baseline for the first variable}{

The first variable is compared with a model that has no predictors,
recorded as step 0 of \code{history}.  Which model that is depends on the
learner, and \code{baseline$type} says which was used:
\tabular{lll}{
\code{baseline$type} \tab The baseline \tab How the first variable is
tested \cr
\code{"null model"} \tab \code{fit_fn(train_sf, character(0))}, when the
learner can fit it \tab at step 1, like any other step \cr
\code{"null_fn"} \tab the model \code{null_fn(train_sf)} returns, for a
learner that cannot fit an empty set \tab at step 1 \cr
\code{"fold mean"} \tab the mean response of each fold's training rows,
when neither is available (\code{\link{fit_rf_model}()} refuses an
empty predictor set) \tab the whole set at the end of the path, then
the set without its first variable
}
Under a fold-mean baseline the first variable is provisional: it is added
without a test, the sweep continues by the rule, and the finished set is
kept only if it beats the fold mean by the same rule; otherwise nothing is
selected, and \code{baseline$path} holds the set that was reached.  The
test is at the end of the path because a forest on a single variable can
lose to the mean when two variables together beat it clearly.  A kept
path of two or more variables is then scored once more without its first
variable (one more set, \code{k} more fits), and that variable is dropped
from \code{selected} when the set is no better with it by the rule and the
rest of the path still beats the fold mean by the rule.
\code{baseline$first} records the test, and \code{selected} is
\code{baseline$path[-1]} after a drop.  When the set is no better with the
first variable but the rest of the path does not beat the fold mean
either, the two cannot be told apart: the set is kept whole, a caution is
logged, and \code{baseline$first$note} says the variable is unproven.  So
in a selection of two or more variables from a learner that falls back to
the fold mean, \code{selected[1]} has been shown to improve the set, not
to predict on its own.

For a GWR the fold mean is a weak baseline: every GWR carries a local
intercept and beats a global mean on any smooth response, whatever its
predictors are.  The baseline that matters is the kernel-weighted local
mean at the same bandwidth, a GWR with a local intercept and no
predictors, which \code{\link{fit_gwr_model}()} fits when its
\code{predictor_vars} is \code{character(0)}.  A \code{fit_fn} that hands
its variables to \code{fit_gwr_model()} therefore gets it as the null
model.  Give such a \code{fit_fn} a fixed \code{bandwidth}, so that every
set is fitted under the kernel the baseline has; with
\code{bandwidth = NULL} each set, the empty one included, searches for
its own.  A GWR \code{fit_fn} that refuses an empty set falls back to the
fold mean, and the sweep logs a caution: read a selection of one variable
made that way as unproven, or supply the local mean as \code{null_fn}, as
the second half of the example does.

\code{predict()} on a GWR warns about held-out locations it cannot
estimate and about those beyond the reach of a fold's fit (classes
\code{"spatialkit_gwr_not_estimable"} and
\code{"spatialkit_gwr_extrapolation"}; see \link{spatial_fit-predict}).
The sweep raises each once, under this function's name, with the totals
over the candidate sets it scored; every set is predicted on the same
rows, so a location can be counted once per set.
}

\section{Selection with a random forest}{

A forest confounds selection with tuning: a predictor that carries no
signal still changes how the trees split, and with ranger's default node
size that can regularise the forest and lower the cross-validated error.
No acceptance rule refuses that gain, so a variable in a forest selection
may be there as a tuning effect, wherever in the order it was added.
Setting the node size in \code{fit_fn} reduces this without removing it,
and comparing the selected set with a tuned forest on the variables you
have other reasons to believe in shows how much of the gain is tuning:
\preformatted{
fit_fn <- function(train_sf, vars)
  fit_rf_model(train_sf, "z", vars, min_node_size = 20)
}
}

\section{Selection score}{

\code{$score} is the cross-validated \code{metric} of the winning set at
the final step: the best of every candidate set the sweep scored.  A
number optimised over many candidates is optimistically biased by
construction, and Cawley and Talbot (2010) show the bias can exceed the differences between
the models being compared.  Quote it as the selection criterion, not as
the performance of the selected model.  A performance estimate needs data
the selection never saw.  Two ways to get one: run this function inside
the \code{fit_fn} of \code{\link{cv_spatial}()}, so the outer folds score a
model whose predictors were chosen on the inner ones alone; or pass
\code{select_on = "split"}, which selects on one spatial half of
\code{train_sf} and returns the selected set's score on the other as
\code{score_holdout}.
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (requireNamespace("sp", quietly = TRUE)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
library(sf)
set.seed(1)
n <- 120
pts <- st_as_sf(
  data.frame(x = 5e5 + runif(n, 0, 1000), y = 5e6 + runif(n, 0, 1000),
             a = rnorm(n), b = rnorm(n), noise = rnorm(n)),
  coords = c("x", "y"), crs = 32632
)
pts$resp <- 3 * pts$a + 2 * pts$b + rnorm(n, 0, 0.3)

# The learner: a GWR with an adaptive bisquare kernel reaching the 30th
# nearest training point.  With no variables it is the kernel-weighted
# local mean, which is the baseline: a GWR's local intercept beats a
# global mean on any smooth response, so the first variable has to beat
# the local mean under the same kernel.
fit_fn <- function(tr, vars) fit_gwr_model(tr, "resp", vars, bandwidth = 30)
sel <- select_features_forward(pts, "resp", c("a", "b", "noise"), fit_fn,
                               k = 3, quiet = TRUE)
sel$selected
sel$baseline$type        # "null model": step 1 was tested against it
sel$history[sel$history$step <= 1, c("step", "variable", "score", "gain")]

# A learner that cannot fit an empty set takes its no-predictor model
# through `null_fn`.  Here the same local mean, written as a spatial_fit
# of its own with a predict() method, for a learner that insists on a
# variable.  Without `null_fn` that sweep would start from the mean of
# each training fold.
needs_one <- function(tr, vars) {
  if (!length(vars)) stop("this learner needs a predictor")
  fit_gwr_model(tr, "resp", vars, bandwidth = 30)
}
local_mean <- function(tr, bandwidth = 30) {
  new_spatial_fit("local_mean_fit",
                  engine = list(xy = st_coordinates(tr), y = tr$resp,
                                bandwidth = bandwidth),
                  formula = resp ~ 1, response_var = "resp",
                  predictor_vars = character(0), data_sf = tr)
}
predict.local_mean_fit <- function(object, newdata = NULL, ...) {
  e  <- object$engine
  at <- if (is.null(newdata)) e$xy else st_coordinates(newdata)
  vapply(seq_len(nrow(at)), function(i) {
    d <- sqrt((e$xy[, 1] - at[i, 1])^2 + (e$xy[, 2] - at[i, 2])^2)
    h <- sort(d)[min(e$bandwidth, length(d))]  # the kernel's reach here
    w <- ifelse(d > h, 0, (1 - d^2 / h^2)^2)   # bisquare weights
    sum(w * e$y) / sum(w)
  }, numeric(1))
}
registerS3method("predict", "local_mean_fit", predict.local_mean_fit)
sel2 <- select_features_forward(pts, "resp", c("a", "b", "noise"), needs_one,
                                k = 3, quiet = TRUE, null_fn = local_mean)
sel2$baseline$type       # "null_fn"
all.equal(sel2$baseline$score, sel$baseline$score)  # the same baseline
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
\references{
Breiman, L., Friedman, J. H., Olshen, R. A. and Stone, C. J. (1984).
\emph{Classification and Regression Trees}. Wadsworth.

Cawley, G. C. and Talbot, N. L. C. (2010). On over-fitting in model
selection and subsequent selection bias in performance evaluation.
\emph{Journal of Machine Learning Research}, 11, 2079--2107.
\url{https://jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html}
}
